build/

# Nothing under third_party/ is ours: metal-cpp is cloned and miniz downloaded,
# both by one command the CMake error message prints when they are missing.
# Vendoring them into history would bury the project's own code under someone
# else's and make "what did we write" unanswerable.
third_party/
.DS_Store
# clangd index, generated from compile_commands.json
.cache/
# Bytecode for the reverse-engineering helpers in tools/re/, which import each other.
__pycache__/

# A git worktree lives here — never commit a worktree inside its own repo.
.claude/

# Screenshots ARE committed, as downscaled JPEGs — a reader should not have to
# build the project to see what it renders, and "regenerable in principle" is
# no help to someone browsing the repo. The full-resolution PNGs the renderer
# emits stay local: five times the bytes for detail nobody sees at README
# width, and `--screenshot` reproduces them exactly.
#
# Benchmark CSVs are committed for the opposite reason: they are measurements
# of a particular machine on a particular day and cannot be reproduced at all.
docs/images/*.png

# Machine-specific content paths, if you keep them in a file rather than the
# environment. The two games recoil-metal reads live wherever they live, and one
# of them is on a removable drive — see the Makefile.
local.mk

# The AI corpora the adapters are written against (ADR-039), fetched by
# `make ai` at pinned commits. Ignored for the reason third_party/ is — 16 MB
# and a thousand files of someone else's code would bury ours — and for one
# more: these trees are never edited, and a copy that does not exist in history
# is a copy nobody can quietly edit. vendor/README.md and vendor/NOTICE.md ARE
# committed; they are what says which versions and on whose terms.
vendor/ai/

# A local mirror of the Supreme Commander Fandom wiki, fetched through api.php
# (its HTML pages 403 any client). Behaviour and role descriptions the blueprints
# do not carry — what a unit is FOR, what counters it, why a hover tank ignores
# torpedoes. Never committed: it is someone else's text under CC-BY-SA, it is
# `WEB`-tier evidence that must never be cited without blueprint or EXE support,
# and it is regenerable by one script.
reference/
